  "title": "Rains, Roads & Politics at the Kinyozi",
  "category": "social",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "level": 9,
  "order": 903,
  "theme": "news",
  "culturalNote": "The kinyozi (barber shop) is Kenya's open-air newsroom: weather, prices, football and politics, all while you get a haircut. Reporting what \"they say\" uses kwamba and reported speech: \"wanasema kwamba barabara itajengwa\" (they say that the road will be built). Kenyans love to hedge politely about politics with \"sijui\", \"huenda\" (maybe) and \"tutaona\" (we'll see). The long rains (masika) bring floods; the short rains (vuli) come in October to December.",
  "startingPoint": "Saturday. You're in the chair at your local kinyozi. Baraka, the barber, starts chatting.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "continuous-tenses",
    "conjunctions-kwamba",
    "conditional-nge"
  ],
